//! lotus/stereo_duplicate.rye — ALES120: stereo_duplicate — the growth gesture carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES112 named `duplicate` — insert a second copy of the
//! `count` samples starting at `start` immediately after the span, IN PLACE, so the clip GROWS by exactly `count` — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two
//! Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries duplicate into stereo the way
//! ALES117 carried cut, ALES118 crop, and ALES119 move: run ALES112's proven mono `duplicate` on each channel with the SAME `start` and `count`, so the repeat lands identically in both
//! and the stereo image stays balanced. Where stereo_cut removes a span and stereo_crop keeps only a span, stereo_duplicate REPEATS one span — the growth side of the stereo edit family.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_duplicate(sc, start, count) inserts a second copy of [start, start+count) immediately after the span in both channels, reusing ALES112's mono
//! `duplicate` per channel over its public API. BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — because mono duplicate can fault only with BadRange (a span outside the samples)
//! or ClipFull (a grow past the fixed buffer) and both channels enter equal-length over the same fixed buffer, the three checks are made once against the shared length up front, so
//! once they pass each mono duplicate is pre-validated to succeed and a refusal never desynchronises the channels. It reuses ALES2's EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO DUPLICATE LAW: the left channel equals mono duplicate(left, start, count) and the right equals mono duplicate(right, start, count), each byte for
//! byte, both grown by count. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit, both grown by exactly count, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVERSE LAW:
//! stereo_duplicate(start, count) then ALES117's stereo_cut(end, count) — end = start+count — returns both channels to the original byte for byte, the inserted copy removed exactly.
//! THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count 0 the identity on both, a bad range BadRange, a grow past the
//! buffer ClipFull.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES120.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-020930_fill-ales120-lotus-stereo-duplicate.md
//! Stands on: lotus/duplicate.rye (ALES112) — the mono growth gesture run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance
//! invariant; lotus/stereo_cut.rye (ALES117) — the stereo span-remove imported by the selftest to prove the inverse law; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its max_clip bound, and
//! the EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It opens a gap in each channel through the proven mono
//! duplicate and fills it with a copy of an existing span — it invents no sample, changes no value it keeps, and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no
//! network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_duplicate.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const duplicate = @import("duplicate.rye");
const stereo_cut = @import("stereo_cut.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_duplicate_version = "20260815.020930";

/// stereo_duplicate forwards ALES2's OWN faults reused by name — BadRange (a span outside the samples) and ClipFull (a grow past the fixed buffer) — and invents none of its own.
pub const StereoDuplicateError = timeline.EditError;

/// Insert a second copy of the `count` samples starting at `start` immediately after the span in both channels in lockstep, the stereo image staying balanced and each channel growing
/// by exactly `count`. Both channels are validated before either is mutated: because mono duplicate can fault only with BadRange or ClipFull and the channels enter equal-length over
/// the same fixed buffer, the three checks are made once against the shared length up front, so a refusal never leaves one channel grown and the other not. Reuses ALES112's mono
/// duplicate per channel.
pub fn stereo_duplicate(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoDuplicateError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the (shared) current samples, checked without underflow and refused by name before any write — the same two guards ALES112's duplicate
    //            keeps, run once against the common length so a refusal lands before either channel mutates.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: the grow of `count` samples fits within the fixed buffer for both channels alike (equal length, equal max_clip), refused ClipFull before any write, checked without
    //            underflow — count against the free seats that remain.
    if (count > timeline.max_clip - len) return error.ClipFull;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono duplicate is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try duplicate.duplicate(&sc.left, start, count);
    try duplicate.duplicate(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — each grew by the same span, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO DUPLICATE LAW — each channel equals ALES112's mono duplicate on that channel byte for byte, both grown by count; THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — left.len ==
//     right.len after, both grown by count; THE INVERSE LAW — duplicate then ALES117's stereo_cut(end, count) returns both channels; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves
//     both channels untouched, count 0 the identity, a grow past the buffer ClipFull ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO DUPLICATE LAW — each channel equals ALES112's mono duplicate on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_duplicate(&sc, 1, 2); // repeat {20,30} on the left, {21,31} on the right

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try duplicate.duplicate(&ml, 1, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try duplicate.duplicate(&mr, 1, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {10,20,30,20,30,40,50} on the left, its per-channel twin on the right; both grown by 2.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 20, 30, 40, 50 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 21, 31, 41, 51 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — the channels stay equal length and each grows by exactly count; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at the head and through the tail.
    {
        var head = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&head, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_duplicate(&head, 0, 2); // duplicate the head
        if (head.left.len != head.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;
        if (head.left.len != 5) return error.HeadLength;
        if (!eq(head.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 1, 2, 3 })) return error.HeadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(head.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 6, 7, 8 })) return error.HeadRightWrong;

        var tail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&tail, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_duplicate(&tail, 1, 2); // duplicate through the tail
        if (tail.left.len != tail.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(tail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 2, 3 })) return error.TailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(tail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 7, 8 })) return error.TailRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE INVERSE LAW — stereo_duplicate(start, count) then ALES117's stereo_cut(end, count) — end = start+count — returns both channels to the original byte for byte.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41 };
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_duplicate(&sc, 1, 2); // {10,20,30,20,30,40} both channels
        try stereo_cut.stereo_cut(&sc, 3, 2); // remove the inserted copy at end = 1+2 = 3
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.InverseLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.InverseRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for a span past the samples and a grow past the fixed buffer.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_duplicate(&sc, 2, 3)) |_| return error.SpanNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;

        // A grow past the fixed buffer refuses ClipFull before any write — fill both channels to their bound, then a duplicate that would grow past it must refuse, both untouched.
        var full = pan.StereoClip{};
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < timeline.max_clip) : (i += 1) {
            try timeline.splice(&full.left, i, &[_]i16{1});
            try timeline.splice(&full.right, i, &[_]i16{2});
        }
        const full_len = full.left.len;
        if (stereo_duplicate(&full, 0, 1)) |_| return error.FullNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.ClipFull) return error.WrongFullError;
        }
        if (full.left.len != full_len) return error.RefusedGrewLeft;
        if (full.right.len != full_len) return error.RefusedGrewRight;
        if (full.left.len != full.right.len) return error.RefusedFullUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count 0 the identity on both channels.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_duplicate(&zero, 1, 0); // duplicate nothing — the identity
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-duplicate: STEREO_DUPLICATE — the growth gesture carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Insert a second copy of [start, start+count) immediately " ++
        "after the span in both channels, reusing ALES112's mono duplicate per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold " ++
        "the same length, so repeating a bar carries it in both speakers at once. Where stereo_cut (ALES117) removes a span and stereo_crop (ALES118) keeps only a span, " ++
        "stereo_duplicate repeats one span — the growth side of the stereo edit family. BOTH channels are validated before either is mutated — because mono duplicate can fault only " ++
        "with BadRange or ClipFull and the channels enter equal-length over the same fixed buffer, the three checks are made once against the shared length up front, so a refusal " ++
        "never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO DUPLICATE LAW: each channel equals ALES112's mono duplicate on that channel byte for byte, both grown by " ++
        "count. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit, both grown by count, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVERSE LAW: duplicate(start, count) then " ++
        "ALES117's stereo_cut(end, count) returns both channels to the original. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still " ++
        "balanced, count 0 the identity on both, a grow past the buffer ClipFull. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips grown through the proven mono duplicate, reading no byte past " ++
        "either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
